[[File:John Frederick Lewis 004.jpg|thumb|''The Coffee Bearer'' by [[John Frederick Lewis]] (1857)]]
[[File:Coffeepot MET DP103144.jpg|thumb|[[Coffeepot (François-Thomas Germain)|Coffeepot]], by French silversmith [[François-Thomas Germain]], 1757, silver with ebony handle, [[Metropolitan Museum of Art]]]]
The '''history of coffee''' spans many centuries. Wild [[coffee plants]] originated in [[Ethiopia]], while the beverage itself has its roots in [[Yemen]], where it was harvested, roasted, and brewed; [[Zawiya (institution)|Sufi Muslims]] in the 15th century used it to aid concentration during night prayers.<ref name="ukers">{{Cite book |last=Ukers |first=William Harrison |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=4O_RAAAAMAAJ |title=All About Coffee (revised 1935) |date=1922 |publisher=Tea and Coffee Trade Journal Company}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=GSw7AAAAIAAJ |title=Johns Hopkins University Studies in Historical and Political Science |date=1967 |publisher=[[Johns Hopkins University Press]] |location=Baltimore |page=25 |ref=none}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Elzebroek |first=A. T. G. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=YvU1XnUVxFQC |title=Guide to Cultivated Plants |date=2008 |publisher=[[CAB International|CABI]] |isbn=978-1-84593-356-2 |location=Wallingford, UK |page=7 |ref=none}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Quickel |first=Anthony T. |chapter=Cairo and Coffee in the Transottoman Trade Network |date=2021 |title=Transottoman Matters |isbn=978-3-8471-1168-9 |pages=84–85 |doi=10.14220/9783737011686.83 |quote=Thus, Hattox argues that it is possible to conclude, on the basis of no earlier references in the historical sources, that coffee was first introduced into the Islamicate lands from Yemen in the mid-fifteenth century.}}</ref><ref>{{Cite thesis |last=Sweetser |first=Heather Marie |title=A Chapter in the History of Coffee: A Critical Edition and Translation of Murtaḍā az-Zabı̄dı̄'s Epistle on Coffee |date=2012 |type=Master's thesis |publisher=The Ohio State University |page=8}}</ref><ref name="CambridgeFood"> {{cite book |title=The Cambridge World History of Food |chapter=Coffee (III.4) |editor-first=Kenneth F. |editor-last=Kiple |editor2-first=Kriemhild |editor2-last=Ornelas |publisher=Cambridge University Press |date=2000 |quote="Coffea arabica first appeared natively in Ethiopia, yet the berries went largely ignored before Arabs in Yemen used them to brew a drink." }} </ref><ref name="OREFoodStudies2024-roast2">{{cite encyclopedia |encyclopedia=Oxford Research Encyclopedia of Food Studies |publisher=Oxford University Press |editor=Goldstein, D|url=https://oxfordre.com/foodstudies/display/10.1093/acrefore/9780197762530.001.0001/acrefore-9780197762530-e-25 |access-date=4 December 2025 |last=Tuchscherer |first=Michel |title=The Ethiopian and Yemeni Roots of Coffee |date=18 September 2024 |doi=10.1093/acrefore/9780197762530.013.25|location=New York |isbn=978-0-19-785253-8 }}</ref>

From Yemen, [[coffee]] spread to [[Mecca]] and the wider Arabian Peninsula, and by the early 16th century it had reached [[Cairo]], [[Damascus]], and [[Istanbul]]. Debates over its permissibility arose in Muslim society, but it soon became a central part of urban social life. 

Through Mediterranean trade routes, coffee entered Europe in the mid-16th century through Italy and other Mediterranean regions. [[Coffeehouse|Coffee houses]] were established in Western Europe by the late 17th century, especially in [[Netherlands|Holland]], [[England]], and [[Germany]]. 

Up to the end of the 17th century, the Yemeni port of [[Mocha, Yemen|Mocha]] was the world’s sole gateway for coffee, but as demand grew, cultivation spread to other parts of the world.<ref name=":2">{{Cite web |title=History of coffee {{!}} Origin, Spread, Ethiopia, Arabia, Facts, & Timeline {{!}} Britannica |url=https://www.britannica.com/topic/history-of-coffee |access-date=7 October 2025 |website=www.britannica.com |language=en |quote=Until the close of the 17th century the world’s limited supply of coffee was obtained almost entirely from the province of Yemen in southern Arabia. But with the increasing popularity of the beverage, the propagation of the plant spread rapidly to Java and other islands of the Indonesian archipelago in the 17th century and to the Americas in the 18th century. Coffee cultivation was started in the Hawaiian Islands in 1825.}}</ref> One of the earliest cultivations of coffee in the New World was when [[Gabriel de Clieu]] brought coffee seedlings to [[Martinique]] in 1720, from which it spread to other Caribbean islands such as [[Saint-Domingue]] and also to [[New Spain|Mexico]]. By 1788, Saint-Domingue supplied half the world's coffee.<ref name="Pendergrast_2001" /> 

[[File:Coffeepot (cafetière "campanienne") (part of a service) MET DT5506.jpg|thumb|Coffeepot (cafetière "campanienne"), part of a service (1836), hard-paste porcelain, Metropolitan Museum of Art]]

By 1852, [[Brazil]] became the world's largest producer of coffee and has held that status ever since. Since 1950, several other major producers emerged, notably [[Colombia]], [[Ivory Coast]], [[Ethiopia]], and [[Vietnam]]; the latter overtook Colombia and became the second-largest producer in 1999.

Today, coffee is one of the world's most popular beverages, with a significant cultural and economic impact globally.

