'''Wet chemistry''' is a form of [[analytical chemistry]] that uses classical methods such as observation to analyze materials. The term ''wet chemistry'' is used as most analytical work is done in the liquid phase.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Trusova |first1=Elena A. |last2=Vokhmintcev |first2=Kirill V. |last3=Zagainov |first3=Igor V. |year=2012 |title=Wet-chemistry processing of powdery raw material for high-tech ceramics |journal=Nanoscale Research Letters |volume=7 |issue=1 |pages=11 |doi=10.1186/1556-276X-7-58 |pmid=22221657 |pmc=3275523 |bibcode=2012NRL.....7...58T |doi-access=free }}</ref> Wet chemistry is also known as ''bench chemistry'', since many tests are performed at lab benches.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Godfrey |first1=Alexander G. |last2=Michael |first2=Samuel G. |last3=Sittampalam |first3=Gurusingham Sitta |last4=Zahoránszky-Köhalmi |first4=Gergely |date=2020 |title=A Perspective on Innovating the Chemistry Lab Bench |journal=Frontiers in Robotics and AI |volume=7 |pages=24 |doi=10.3389/frobt.2020.00024 |issn=2296-9144 |pmc=7805875 |pmid=33501193|doi-access=free }}</ref>

