The '''MIT Department of Physics''' has over 120 faculty members, is often cited as the largest physics department in the United States, and hosts top-ranked programs.<ref>{{cite web |title= Largest Physics PhD-Granting Departments in the U.S.|url=https://www.aip.org/statistics/data-graphics/largest-physics-phd-granting-departments-us |website=Largest Physics PhD-Granting Departments in the U.S. |date=14 April 2014 |publisher=American Institute of Physics |accessdate=15 July 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Best Physics Schools, 2018 |url=https://www.usnews.com/best-graduate-schools/top-science-schools/physics-rankings |publisher=US News |accessdate=15 July 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=2019 Best Colleges for Physics in America |url=https://www.niche.com/colleges/search/best-colleges-for-physics/ |publisher=Niche |accessdate=15 July 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Top Universities for Physics in 2019 |url=https://www.topuniversities.com/university-rankings-articles/university-subject-rankings/top-universities-physics-2019 |publisher=Quacquarelli Symonds |accessdate=16 July 2019}}</ref> It offers the [[Bachelor of Science|SB]], [[Master of Science|SM]], [[PhD]], and [[ScD]] degrees. Fourteen alumni of the department and nine current or former faculty members (two of whom were also students at MIT) have won the [[Nobel Prize in Physics]].The Department of Physics was born when MIT founder William Barton Rogers proposed in 1865 to bring their Mens et Manus philosophy to life by creating a new laboratory of physics and mechanics in another department’s back room.<ref>{{Cite web | title=History of MIT Physics » MIT Physics | url=https://physics.mit.edu/about-physics/our-history/ | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210226215429/https://physics.mit.edu/about-physics/our-history/ | access-date=2026-09-04 | archive-date=2021-02-26}}</ref>

